## Driver Optimization

Cache drivers are added to the generated app on demand.

Named generated accessors such as `app.Caches().Sessions()` do not return errors. They represent generated configuration invariants from the env state present when `forj generate --cache` last ran. If one of those accessors is missing at runtime, the generated code and the current env configuration are out of sync, so the accessor fails fast.

When you run:

```bash
forj generate --cache
```

GoForj inspects:

- `CACHE_*` for the default cache
- `CACHE_<NAME>_*` for named caches

Then it generates cache config code that imports only the optional drivers enabled by `CACHE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS`. If that env var is unset, generation falls back to the currently active `CACHE_*` and `CACHE_<NAME>_*` driver selections.

Examples:

- memory-only apps pull only the root `github.com/goforj/cache` module
- memory + file apps still use only the root cache module
- apps that add Memcached pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/memcachedcache` only when needed
- apps that add DynamoDB pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/dynamocache` only when needed
- apps that add SQLite pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/sqlitecache` only when needed
- apps that add Postgres pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/postgrescache` only when needed
- apps that add MySQL pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/mysqlcache` only when needed
- apps that add NATS pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/natscache` only when needed
- apps that add Redis pull `github.com/goforj/cache/driver/rediscache` only when needed

If a runtime cache selects a driver that is not listed in `CACHE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS`, generation fails fast with a validation error.

`forj build` also runs cache generation automatically before building the app binary.

## Switching Drivers

`CACHE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS` is the set of drivers built into the app; `CACHE_DRIVER` and the named `CACHE_<NAME>_DRIVER` values select which of them are active. To switch an existing cache to a driver already built in, configure its connection and required service, change the active driver, then restart or redeploy the App. No regeneration or rebuild is needed.

To use a driver that is not built in, add it to `CACHE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS`, run `forj generate --cache`, and build a new artifact before selecting it. Cache entries are not copied between backends, so expect a cold cache unless you migrate or warm the data separately.

## Cache Shell

Generated Apps include `cache:shell`, with the short alias `cache`, for opening a shell against configured Redis cache stores.

Open the default Redis cache, or the only configured Redis cache when the default cache is not shellable:

```bash
forj cache
```

Use the canonical command when you want the full name:

```bash
forj cache:shell
```

Named cache stores use the App-facing cache name:

```bash
forj cache sessions
forj cache --store sessions
```

By default, `forj cache` tries `redis-cli` first. If the local client is missing, it falls back to the generated Docker Compose `redis` service when one exists.

Choose the method explicitly:

```bash
forj cache --method local
forj cache --method compose
```

Use `--print` to inspect the command GoForj will run. Secrets are masked:

```bash
forj cache --print
forj cache sessions --method local --print
```

Run one Redis command non-interactively:

```bash
forj cache --exec "PING"
forj cache sessions --exec "GET user:1"
```

Use `--` to pass client-native arguments directly after GoForj adds the configured connection arguments:

```bash
forj cache -- PING
forj cache sessions -- GET user:1
forj cache --store sessions -- SCAN 0 MATCH 'session:*'
```

## Usage

```go
err := app.Cache().SetString("health", "ok", 0)
```

Resolve a named cache:

```go
sessions := app.Caches().Sessions()
limits := app.Caches().RateLimits()
```

## Notes

- File cache paths default to a temp dir when `FILE_DIR` is not set.
- Named file caches default to isolated subdirectories under the temp cache root.
- Run `forj generate --cache` after adding or renaming named caches when you want to refresh cache code directly.
- `forj generate` also regenerates cache accessors and optional cache driver imports.
- `forj build` runs the same cache generation step automatically before building.
